{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-135366" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the inverse of the following matrix:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070476/state.json" "file_sha256" "bb319241702f26d3134acb0ff79440ad1b22e94950248c5cb08fa2f27c10db76"} {"question" "q-135366" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The given matrix can be obtained from the identity matrix by permuting its columns. Therefore, $A$ is a permutation matrix.\n\nThe inverse of a permutation matrix can be found as follows: \n\n$$\n{P}^{-1}={P}^{T}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1}={[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}={[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070476/state.json" "file_sha256" "bb319241702f26d3134acb0ff79440ad1b22e94950248c5cb08fa2f27c10db76"} {"question" "q-135366" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070476/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e995597b22ac8c67939a86558392bc9717625f07bdb3494f044688b5cd86a273"} {"question" "q-135366" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070476/state.json" "file_sha256" "bb319241702f26d3134acb0ff79440ad1b22e94950248c5cb08fa2f27c10db76"} {"question" "q-135366" "field" "selection"